Add some tolerance to BuilderUtil.validateCharSetEncoding() I currently try to use a given web service which returns "inconsistent" responses concerning the encoding setup: The HTTP header is set and claims to head an ISO-8859-1 response, but the XML content itself claims to be of UTF-8. As far as I can see this causes my Axis2 1.4.1 client (generated based on xmlbeans 2.3) to throw the following exception: org.apache.axis2.AxisFault: Character Set Encoding from transport information [ISO-8859-1] does not match with character set encoding in the received SOAP message [UTF-8] [...] The response itself looks like: "HTTP/1.1 200 OK[][
]" "HTTP/1.1 200 OK[][
]" "Date: Tue, 16 Jun 2009 15:02:40 GMT[][
]" "Server: Apache-Coyote/1.1[][
]" "X-Powered-By: Servlet 2.4; Tomcat-5.0.28/JBoss-3.2.6 (build: CVSTag=JBoss_3_2_6 date=200410140106)[][
]" "Content-Type: text/xml;charset=ISO-8859-1[][
]" "Content-Length: 475[][
]" "[][
]" " [
]" " soapenv:Client Error in parsing Content is not allowed in prolog. " (as extracted from logs generated by "org.apache.commons.httpclient.Wire wire"). Formally thats OK, but in real world it is all about interoperability, and that is: about tolerance, as far as its clear what is "meant". Thus it would be nice to be able to deliberately "weaken" the validation process especially for the encoding: It should be possible to switch of the check of XML encoding setup against HTTP header encoding setup and instead simply use the XML encoding setup. Especially if a given remote service must be used, the client code implementer has no influence on what the service returns -- she simply has to arrange her code to match the given service. And in my case, this simply seems impossible with the released Axis2 1.4.1 client code. I think it's wrong to say that interoperability is about tolerance. Also, if the service returns two conflicting pieces of information it is not clear at all what is "meant". Therefore I don't think we should modify Axis2 to cope with this particular type of broken service. Anyway, this is actually not necessary, because you can create your own replacement for org.apache.axis2.builder.SOAPBuilder (which would be more lenient and work with the broken service) and register it in axis2.xml. Custom SOAPBilder sounds fine. How is that achieved? Where is documented how to implement and deploy such a nice thing? Thank you for information. Also, own more axis2.xml. JRE "inconsistent" claims be of (generated the org.apache.axis2.builder.BuilderUtil.validateCharSetEncoding(BuilderUtil .j org.apache.axis2.builder.SOAPBuilder.processDocument(SOAPBuilder.java:57 ) org.apache.axis2.transport.TransportUtils.createDocumentElement(Transpor tU org.apache.axis2.transport.TransportUtils.createSOAPMessage(TransportUti ls org.apache.axis2.transport.TransportUtils.createSOAPMessage(TransportUti ls org.apache.axis2.description.OutInAxisOperationClient.handleResponse(Out In org.apache.axis2.description.OutInAxisOperationClient.send(OutInAxisOper at org.apache.axis2.description.OutInAxisOperationClient.executeImpl(OutInA xi org.apache.axis2.client.OperationClient.execute(OperationClient.java:163 ) xmlns:xsi=" lt "org.apache.commons.httpclient.Wire interoperability, validation of has For the moment this is very poorly documented. See AXIS2-2905. There is some basic information in [1]. [1] That does not provide very much guidance, indeed. So I have to ask again :-( Do I interpret it right then that I could subclass the "usual" Message Builder (which fails), implement it with a weaker encoding check, and "register" it in axis2.xml as a custom message builder for lets say contentType="text/xml;charset=ISO-8859-1" as it is returned in the http header by the service my application must talk to? That is correct, except that you can only register the message builder for "text/xml", not for "text/xml;charset=ISO-8859-1" (Axis2 removes any parameters when doing the lookup of the message builder). Fortunately the web service in question is currently the only one my application has to talk to -- but as far as I can see this "general" content type will cause all "usual" web services called via this configuration being processed by my custom message builder -- right? Or is the content type "text/xml" already somewhat specific (and not simply the "usual default type")? (This question may also be useful for other readers of this thread having to deal with different web services to be called from one application.) "text/xml" is the content type for SOAP 1.1. So all SOAP messages will be parsed using your new builder. Therefore you must make sure that this implementation gives the same result as the original one for messages received from standards compliant services. (That should be no problem since your initial proposal was to change Axis2...) (I have to admit: This one is only slightly related to the current topic, but...) The Axis2-built XML of the request starts with: This led to the following error on the server side (it was correctly enveloped as XML response.): Content is not allowed in prolog. A support person corrected the requests XML header to: and responses from their server start with Why the heck does Axis2 generate *single* quotes as XML value delimiter for the XML header instead of *double* quotes? Inside the "XML content" *double* quotes are used! How can I configure that right? (Sorry for posting this a bit off topic.) While double quotes are used most of the time, the XML spec also allow single quotes. The service you are trying to talk to seems really broken! I'm not aware of a way to configure this. It is actually not Axis2 that generates this, but the StAX parser, which by default is Woodstox. You should have a look at the available documentation of Woodstox or post this question on their mailing list. Marking this issue as resolved. If there are any further questions, please use the axis-user mailing list.

